        {
          "source_quote": "ἔπειτα αὐτὸ ἐν ἑαυτῷ κυηθέν",
          "english_quote": "Then, having developed within itself",
          "issue": "The general verb ‘developed’ obscures the specific gestational image in κυηθέν. The source describes this body as gestated within itself, continuing the surrounding conception and birth imagery.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use ‘Then, having been gestated within itself’. This preserves the unusual self-gestation rather than explaining it away.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὑπὸ τοῦ παρειληφότος θειώδους πνεύματος ἀναφερόμενον",
          "english_quote": "being lifted by the sulfurous breath it had taken in",
          "issue": "παρειληφότος is an active perfect participle agreeing with πνεύματος: the breath is the receiving or taking agent. The draft instead makes the developing body the agent that took in the breath. Although the preceding clause describes drawing in surrounding breath, that does not translate this additional active-participle claim. The lexical note repeats the reversal without disclosing it. This is a blocking translation error, not a need to verify or alter the printed Greek.",
          "suggested_revision": "A source-supported candidate is ‘being lifted by the sulfurous breath that had received it’. Explain that the developing body is the contextually inferred object of ‘received’; retain the disclosed lexical alternative for θειώδους. Submit the revised wording and note to fresh review.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τῇ περιφερείᾳ τῶν ὠῶν προσεοικὸς καὶ τῷ τάχει τῆς πτήσεως",
          "english_quote": "resembling eggs in its round shape and in the speed of its flight",
          "issue": "The two coordinated dative phrases supply the comparisons: the roundness of eggs and the swiftness of flight. τῶν ὠῶν modifies περιφερείᾳ; it does not also modify πτήσεως. The draft extends the egg comparison to flight, and its notes explicitly characterize the source as referring to an egg's flight. That characterization introduces an unsupported attachment and mislabels a translation-created difficulty as a source feature.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use a source-transparent candidate such as ‘resembling the roundness of eggs and the swiftness of flight’. Revise the lexical and source-issue notes to remove the assertion that the Greek attributes flight to eggs. Do not invent a particular flying creature or mechanism. The replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        }
